digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-55999-4
  rankdir="TB";
  "Deduct_Goal" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Given Existing allotropes have limitations, If limitations exist then seek new forms, therefore Goal is new polymeric forms."];
  "Induct_BlackP_Flaws" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given Black P is unstable, Black P synthesis is high energy, therefore Black P has significant drawbacks."];
  "Induct_Stability" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given Low nuclearity unstable, Polymeric stable, therefore Polymeric forms offer stability."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes investigating the encapsulation and polymerisation of white phosphorus inside the helical channels of the indium-based metal–organic framework MFM-300(In), aiming to crystallographically characterise a stable double-helical phosphorus chain built from butterfly P8 units, relate such motifs to red phosphorus, and assess MOF-confined phosphorus nanostructures with tunable electronic properties.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Together, limitations of known phosphorus allotropes, the plausibility of P8-containing polymeric chains, and the suitability of crystalline porous MOF hosts motivate MOF-confined synthesis and characterization of new phosphorus nanostructures."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) When phosphorus formed in carbon nanotube systems is amorphous, structural determination of the encapsulated phosphorus remains elusive."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Crystalline porous MOFs are plausible hosts for including, stabilizing, and diffraction-characterizing phosphorus chains."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Studying phosphorus chains in MOFs is motivated both by possible improvements in semiconducting properties and by potential structural insight into helical motifs related to red phosphorus."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) A plausible research strategy is to use crystalline porous MOF hosts to investigate P8-containing phosphorus chains."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Synthesis and study of phosphorus chains in MOF hosts is a motivated proposal for pursuing new polymeric phosphorus forms, possible electronic-property tuning, and structural insight into red phosphorus motifs."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) The repaired evidence supports proposing MOF-hosted synthesis and characterization of phosphorus chains as a route to stabilized polymeric phosphorus nanostructures."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Among phosphorus allotropes, the contrast between broadly limited chemical stability and the relative stability of polymeric forms motivates focusing on polymeric phosphorus as a promising stable direction."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Black phosphorus/phosphorene has attractive semiconducting tunability but also significant practical drawbacks from environmental instability and high-energy synthesis."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Black phosphorus and red phosphorus illustrate that selected existing phosphorus allotropes involve property and practicality tradeoffs."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Predicted stability of P8 cages/chains together with isolated fibrous phosphorus containing P8 building blocks makes P8-containing phosphorus chains a scientifically plausible target."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) P8-containing phosphorus chains are a viable target for further synthesis and characterization."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Prior host- or coordination-based chemistry indicates that controlled environments can help stabilize phosphorus chains or nanostructures, motivating host-directed stabilization strategies."];
  "Rule_Goal" [label="(0,0,0) If Existing allotropes have limitations, then seek new polymeric forms."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) Phosphorus has diverse allotropes but few are chemically stable."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently there are predictions that P8 cages/chains are more stable than P4."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) Currently there is isolation of fibrous phosphorus featuring P8 building blocks."];
  "S12" [label="(12,0,0) If supramolecular chemistry is used, it promotes stabilisation of phosphorus chains in porous hosts."];
  "S13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently there is use of CuI to stabilise phosphorus nanorods and anionic species."];
  "S14" [label="(14,0,0) Currently there is adsorption and polymerisation of P4 in carbon nanotubes, but they are amorphous."];
  "S15" [label="(15,0,0) If materials are amorphous, then their structure determination remains elusive."];
  "S16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently there are MOFs with high crystallinity and tuneable porosity ideal for inclusion and diffraction."];
  "S17" [label="(17,0,0) If MOFs are doped with phosphorus chains, then semiconducting properties may improve."];
  "S19" [label="(19,0,0) Observation: Similarity between Raman spectra suggests helical chains in amorphous red phosphorus."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently there is instability in low nuclearity species (P4, P2, P6)."];
  "S3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently there is stability in polymeric forms (Red, Violet, Black)."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently there is Phosphorene (Black P monolayer) as a semiconductor with tuneable bandgap."];
  "S5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently there is instability of Black P sheets towards moisture, oxygen, and light."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently there is high energy consumption during Black P synthesis."];
  "S7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently there is Red Phosphorus which is stable but has a wide bandgap."];
